WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.727
Take the café energy

2
00:00:04.814 --> 00:00:06.901
five x squared plus four

3
00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:08.989
x y plus two y squared

4
00:00:08.989 --> 00:00:11.164
and set it equal to six.

5
00:00:11.164 --> 00:00:15.078
The points where that holds form an ellipse

6
00:00:15.078 --> 00:00:17.340
tilted by the cross term.

7
00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:19.900
Along which directions is

8
00:00:19.988 --> 00:00:23.347
it longest and shortest, and how long?

9
00:00:23.347 --> 00:00:24.850
Pause and guess.

10
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.140
Turn the coordinates to the eigenvectors

11
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.407
x equals Q y.

12
00:00:33.407 --> 00:00:34.945
Q is a rotation

13
00:00:35.036 --> 00:00:37.750
and in the new coordinates the

14
00:00:37.841 --> 00:00:39.831
cross term disappears

15
00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:43.270
six y one squared plus y two squared.

16
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.077
Along the first axis, two, one

17
00:00:46.157 --> 00:00:48.376
six y one squared equals six

18
00:00:48.455 --> 00:00:50.041
gives half-axis one.

19
00:00:50.041 --> 00:00:52.736
Along the second, one, minus two

20
00:00:52.815 --> 00:00:55.193
y two squared equals six gives

21
00:00:55.273 --> 00:00:57.096
the square root of six

22
00:00:57.096 --> 00:00:59.236
about two point four five.

23
00:00:59.236 --> 00:01:02.090
This is the principal axis theorem

24
00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.340
the eigenvectors are the principal axes.

25
00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.881
So each half-axis is the square root

26
00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.364
of six over its eigenvalue.

27
00:01:11.364 --> 00:01:14.378
For x transpose S x equal to one

28
00:01:14.466 --> 00:01:16.682
it is one over the square

29
00:01:16.771 --> 00:01:18.810
root of the eigenvalue.

30
00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:22.002
So the biggest eigenvalue

31
00:01:22.107 --> 00:01:24.510
gives the longest axis?

32
00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.207
The opposite.

33
00:01:26.292 --> 00:01:28.354
Take nine x squared plus

34
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.329
y squared equals four.

35
00:01:30.415 --> 00:01:32.734
The eigenvalue nine belongs

36
00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.452
to the x direction.

37
00:01:34.452 --> 00:01:37.286
But x can only reach two thirds

38
00:01:37.286 --> 00:01:39.090
while y reaches two.

39
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.331
A large eigenvalue means

40
00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.144
the energy grows fast

41
00:01:43.144 --> 00:01:46.296
so the curve stops early: a short axis.

42
00:01:46.375 --> 00:01:48.503
The ellipse is narrow where

43
00:01:48.582 --> 00:01:50.000
the bowl is steep.

44
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.558
In the turned coordinates the form

45
00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:56.449
is lambda one y one squared plus

46
00:01:56.537 --> 00:01:58.727
lambda two y two squared

47
00:01:58.814 --> 00:02:01.617
so the shape depends only on the

48
00:02:01.705 --> 00:02:03.895
signs of the eigenvalues.

49
00:02:03.895 --> 00:02:06.260
Both positive: an ellipse.

50
00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.230
Opposite signs: a hyperbola.

51
00:02:09.324 --> 00:02:13.441
x squared plus six x y plus y squared equals

52
00:02:13.535 --> 00:02:17.278
four has eigenvalues four and minus two.

53
00:02:17.278 --> 00:02:19.804
One positive and one zero

54
00:02:19.804 --> 00:02:24.015
two times x plus two y, squared, equals two

55
00:02:24.015 --> 00:02:27.290
which is a pair of parallel lines.

56
00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.748
For two by two matrices

57
00:02:29.835 --> 00:02:32.470
the determinant is the product

58
00:02:32.558 --> 00:02:34.226
of the eigenvalues

59
00:02:34.314 --> 00:02:36.597
so its sign alone tells an

60
00:02:36.685 --> 00:02:38.880
ellipse from a hyperbola.

61
00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.052
The eigenvectors are the best coordinates

62
00:02:43.052 --> 00:02:46.472
but any invertible change of variables

63
00:02:46.472 --> 00:02:50.235
x equals B y, turns S into B transpose S B.

64
00:02:50.235 --> 00:02:52.715
In the plane that is a warp

65
00:02:52.801 --> 00:02:55.024
the hyperbola is bent into

66
00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.991
a different hyperbola

67
00:02:56.991 --> 00:02:59.300
but never into an ellipse.

68
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.679
Sylvester's law of inertia

69
00:03:01.679 --> 00:03:03.557
the numbers of positive

70
00:03:03.557 --> 00:03:06.863
negative and zero eigenvalues never change.

71
00:03:06.938 --> 00:03:09.117
Completing the square is such

72
00:03:09.192 --> 00:03:10.845
a change of variables

73
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.625
which is why the signs of the pivots

74
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.179
are the signs of the eigenvalues

75
00:03:16.179 --> 00:03:17.757
one and minus eight

76
00:03:17.757 --> 00:03:19.860
against four and minus two.

77
00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.490
Different numbers, the same signs.

78
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.065
Only a rotation keeps the

79
00:03:25.154 --> 00:03:27.201
eigenvalues themselves

80
00:03:27.201 --> 00:03:29.960
and with them the exact shape.

81
00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.482
To sum up.

82
00:03:31.576 --> 00:03:34.214
Turn to the eigenvectors and

83
00:03:34.308 --> 00:03:36.568
the cross term vanishes

84
00:03:36.663 --> 00:03:39.771
each half-axis is the square root

85
00:03:39.865 --> 00:03:42.220
of c over its eigenvalue.

86
00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.720
The signs of the eigenvalues name the shape

87
00:03:45.791 --> 00:03:48.368
and Sylvester's law says those signs

88
00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.730
survive any change of variables.

89
00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:51.744
Next

90
00:03:51.744 --> 00:03:55.216
what if the matrix has complex entries?

91
00:03:55.216 --> 00:03:57.733
Symmetric becomes Hermitian

92
00:03:57.733 --> 00:04:00.510
and the Fourier matrix appears.
